About 66 million years ago, huge volcanic eruptions in western India poured out layer upon layer of [[lava|lava]], now called the [[@deccan|Deccan Traps]]. Over millions of years, that dark rock weathered into black soil, also called *regur*.

Black soil covers much of Maharashtra and parts of Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh and Karnataka. It is full of clay, holds water for a long time and is excellent for [[@clothes-cotton|cotton]], which is why it is also called black cotton soil. In summer it cracks so deeply you could slide a ruler into the gaps.

{{story:earthworms}}

Kalpana was the youngest of four children. As a girl she was drawn to aeroplanes and liked drawing them. After school in Karnal, she joined Punjab Engineering College in Chandigarh in the late 1970s to study aeronautical engineering, the science of [[@airplanes-fly|how aircraft fly]]. She was one of very few women in her course.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

In 1982 she moved to the United States. She earned a master's degree from the University of Texas at Arlington and then a PhD in aerospace engineering from the University of Colorado in 1988. She later became an American citizen.

The computer printed one character every 12 seconds for each radio channel. A blank or a 1 meant almost nothing, just the usual hiss. Numbers went up to 9, and then letters took over for stronger signals. "6EQUJ5" rose to "U", about 30 times louder than the background, and fell again.

It was a perfect rise and fall, shaped like a hill. Big Ear had two receiving horns, side by side, and each should have seen the source a few minutes apart. The signal showed up in only one of them, a puzzle that remains unsolved. Either the source switched off within minutes, or something stranger happened.

{{story:science-fiction}}

Most planets, including Earth, spin in the same direction as they orbit the Sun. Venus spins the other way, and very slowly. If you could cycle at a gentle pace along its [[equator|equator]], you could keep up with the Sun. A Venus year holds fewer than two of its sunrise-to-sunrise days.

Why is it backwards? Nobody knows for sure. One idea is that a giant collision long ago knocked it over. Another is that the Sun's pull on Venus's thick [[atmosphere|atmosphere]] slowly changed its spin over billions of years. Venus's air is so heavy that standing on the surface would feel like being nearly a kilometre deep in the ocean, and it is about 465 °C, hot enough to melt lead.

{{story:day-night}}

Earth races around the Sun at about 30 km every second. The Sun's pull bends that path into a near-circle. If the pull vanished, Earth would carry on straight, like a stone flung from a sling.

It wouldn't crash into anything soon. Space is so empty that Earth could travel for millions of years without hitting another star. The Moon would come along, still circling Earth, because Earth's gravity would still hold it.

{{story:deep-sea-vents}}

A blue whale has no teeth. Instead it has rows of bristly plates called baleen hanging from its upper jaw. To feed, it charges at a cloud of krill and opens its mouth wide. Pleats in its throat stretch like a balloon, and it gulps a mouthful of water about as heavy as its own body.

Then it pushes the water out through the baleen, which works like a sieve. The krill stay inside. Scientists think whales can't grow much bigger because they would struggle to find enough krill to fuel the extra size.

{{story:sauropod-necks}}

The Great Banyan grows in the Acharya Jagadish Chandra Bose Indian Botanic Garden in Howrah, across the river from [[@kolkata|Kolkata]]. It is thought to be [[@trees-oldest|at least 250 years old]]. Two great [[cyclone|cyclones]] in the 1860s tore off some of its big branches. Later, [[fungus|fungus]] rotted the main trunk, and in 1925 gardeners removed it.

The tree hardly noticed. Over 3,000 of its hanging roots had already reached the soil and turned into trunks. Today its crown covers about 1.9 hectares, about two and a half football fields. A road runs around it, and gardeners guide new roots down into the ground with bamboo poles.

{{story:fig-wasps}}

Snow leopards mainly hunt bharal, also called blue sheep, and Himalayan ibex, a wild goat with long curved horns. Both are expert climbers. The snow leopard stalks them from above, then charges downhill, using its powerful back legs to leap across gaps between rocks.

It can bring down animals heavier than itself. After a big kill, it may stay near the [[carcass|carcass]] for several days, feeding slowly. In summer it also catches marmots, big ground squirrels that whistle a warning when they spot danger.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

A [[@cars-engine|car engine]] burns fuel mixed with air to push [[piston|pistons]]. Ethanol burns cleanly and gives off less smoke than petrol, but it holds less energy in each litre, so cars need a little more of it. It also attracts water and can wear out ordinary rubber parts.

That is why blends are labelled. E10 has 10% ethanol, E20 has 20%, and E100 is almost pure ethanol. Ordinary cars are built for low blends. Flex-fuel cars have tougher pipes and seals and smarter computers so they can handle anything up to E100.

{{story:cars-engine}}

Balkrishna Doshi worked for Le Corbusier in Paris and helped on his Indian projects. Back in India, he designed IIM Bangalore and his own studio in Ahmedabad, Sangath, with curved vaults half sunk into the ground to stay cool. His Aranya housing in Indore gave [[@slums-housing|low-income families]] a basic plot and core house that they could extend as they earned more. He won the Pritzker Prize in 2018.

Charles Correa designed the Gandhi Smarak Sangrahalaya at [[@gandhi|Gandhi]]'s Sabarmati Ashram in 1963. It is a cluster of small tiled rooms around open courts and a pool, gentle and modest like the man it remembers. He also designed the Jawahar Kala Kendra in [[@jaipur|Jaipur]] and helped plan Navi Mumbai.

{{story:urban-planning}}

Fresco is a tricky method. A thin layer of fresh plaster is spread on the ceiling each morning. The painter has to paint it while it is still wet, before it dries, usually in a single day. As the plaster dries, the colours become locked into the surface, which is why frescoes can last for centuries.

Each day's patch is called a *giornata*, from the Italian for "a day's work". Experts have counted the joins between these patches to work out how many days Michelangelo spent on different parts. Mistakes were hard to fix. A bad patch had to be chipped off and redone.

{{story:rome-city}}

Fear of thirteen has a long name: triskaidekaphobia. Nobody knows exactly where it began. One idea is that twelve was seen as complete: twelve months, twelve hours on a clock face, twelve signs of the [[zodiac|zodiac]]. Thirteen is one too many. Christian tradition also says 13 people sat down at the Last Supper.

The fear is strong enough to shape buildings. In many American skyscrapers, the button goes from 12 to 14. The 13th floor is still there. It just has a different name. Some streets skip house number 13, and some airlines have no row 13.

{{story:maths-lottery-odds}}

Suppose the plant turned by a simple fraction of a circle, like a half or a third. The seeds would line up in straight spokes, leaving big empty wedges between them. The golden angle is special because it never quite repeats. Each new seed lands in a gap left by the others.

[[botanist|Botanists]] call the arrangement of leaves and seeds phyllotaxis. Pinecones, pineapples and cauliflowers often show the same spiral numbers. A pineapple usually has 8 spirals one way and 13 the other. The same packing rule shows up in plants as different as daisies and the leaves around a stem.

{{story:poetry}}

In India, children have long learned pahade, chanting tables aloud in class. "Do ekam do, do dooni chaar", two ones are two, two twos are four. Many older Indians learned tables up to 20 or beyond, and some learned tables for [[@fractions|fractions]] too, like sawa (1¼), dedh (1½) and dhai (2½).

In Japan, children learn a rhyming chant called kuku, "nine nine", for the tables up to 9 × 9. In England, every child in Year 4, aged 8 or 9, takes an online check of tables up to 12 × 12. In the United States, most children learn tables up to 10 or 12, though how they practise varies a lot between schools.

{{story:brain-works}}

Many tables are just doubles of others. The 4 times table is the 2 times table doubled. The 8 times table is the 4 times table doubled. So for 8 × 7, you can go 2 × 7 = 14, double to 28, double again to 56.

For the 5 times table, multiply by 10 and halve. 5 × 8 is half of 80, which is 40. For the 6 times table, take the 3 times table and double it. These tricks are part of what people call [[@mental-maths|mental maths]], and they build real number sense, not just memory.

{{story:memory}}

Since reacting to the ball is too slow, keepers watch the kicker instead. The angle of the run-up, the position of the hips and where the standing foot points all give clues. Many right-footed kickers find it more natural to shoot to their left.

Goalkeepers and their coaches now study video of every likely penalty taker. In big matches, some keepers carry notes on their water bottles about which way each opponent usually shoots.

{{story:reflexes}}

Nobody knows for sure why we hiccup. One idea, put forward by French scientists in 2003, links it to our distant ancestors. [[@frogs-monsoon|Tadpoles]] and some fish breathe by gulping water over their gills while closing a flap to keep it out of their lungs. That gulp-and-close action looks a lot like a hiccup. The idea is that the brain [[circuit|circuit]] for it may have been passed down, long after our ancestors left the water.

Another idea is that hiccups help babies. Babies hiccup a lot, even before birth. Some scientists think hiccups may help them push swallowed air out of the stomach so there's more room for milk. Both ideas are still argued about, and neither has been proven.

{{story:frogs-monsoon}}

In 1884 Tesla sailed to [[@new-york|New York]] with very little money. He worked for Thomas Edison's company for a few months. Edison's system used direct current, or DC, which flows one way. It was hard to send DC far, so power stations had to be close to homes.

Tesla believed alternating current, which switches direction many times a second, was better. AC can be raised to high voltage for long journeys and lowered again for safe use in homes. The two men disagreed, and Tesla left. Their rivalry, and the wider fight between the [[@tesla-edison|Edison and Westinghouse]] companies, became known as the war of the currents.

{{story:electricity-made}}

On 4 November 1712, Angre's fleet captured an armed yacht called the *Algerine*. It belonged to William Aislabie, the British governor of Bombay. Angre held it for three months. The [[@east-india-company|East India Company]] paid 30,000 rupees in February 1713 to get the yacht and its passengers back.

Picture the scene in Bombay: the most powerful Englishman in western India, and he could not keep his own boat safe in his own harbour. After that, the Company built more warships and spent years trying to break Angre's hold on the coast.

{{story:pirates}}

In the early 1980s, S. R. Rao and the National Institute of Oceanography began searching the waters off Dwarka and nearby Bet Dwarka island. The work was hard. Strong currents stir up mud, and divers often could see only a metre or two in front of them.

They mapped blocks of stone, some dressed into shapes, lying in rough lines several metres under water. Around Bet Dwarka, they found pottery and a seal carved with animal heads. Rao argued the finds matched a late Harappan-age port.

{{story:shipwrecks}}

His son Narasimhavarman I, who ruled from about 630 to 668, was nicknamed Mamalla, "the great wrestler". The port city was named Mamallapuram after him.

He is also remembered for a famous victory. In 642 he defeated the [[@chalukyas|Chalukya]] king Pulakeshin II and captured his capital, Vatapi, today's Badami in Karnataka. During his reign, the Chinese monk Xuanzang visited Kanchipuram and described it as a large city of learning with many [[monastery|monasteries]] and temples.

{{story:tsunami}}

The road was not poured in place over the sea. It was made on land at a casting yard at Worli, in giant concrete blocks called segments. Each one weighed many tonnes.

Barges carried the segments out. A huge steel machine called a launching gantry stood on the piles, lifted each block and held it in position. Steel cables were threaded through all the blocks and pulled tight, squeezing them into one solid road. It works like a row of books that you can lift off a shelf by pressing hard on both ends.

{{story:bridges}}

Eight of the world's fourteen mountains over 8,000 m stand in Nepal or on its borders, including Everest, Kanchenjunga and Annapurna. In 2020, Nepal and China announced together that Everest is 8,848.86 m tall, after a joint survey.

The climb from the Terai to the peaks happens in a very short distance, so Nepal has a bit of everything. There are jungles with tigers and one-horned rhinos in Chitwan National Park, pine forests in the hills, and [[@glaciers|glaciers]] and snow leopards at the top. Walking for a week in Nepal can feel like walking from Kerala to the Arctic.

{{story:mountains-tallest}}

Kumbhalgarh, built mostly in the 1400s by Rana Kumbha of Mewar, has a wall that runs for about 36 km over the hills. In places it is wide enough for several horses to ride side by side. Inside are dozens of temples, wells and palaces.

Chittorgarh, also in Mewar, is one of the largest forts in India, spread over a long hilltop about as big as several hundred football fields put together. It was besieged three times in its long history, and its stories of courage are still told in songs.

{{story:knights-castles}}

Malgudi has a river called Sarayu, a market road, a [[@indian-railways|railway station]], a mission school and a banyan tree. Readers can picture it so clearly that many assume it is real. Narayan said he first thought of the name, and of a railway station, before he had a single character.

The town changes slowly across the books, just as real Indian towns did in the 1900s. Cars, cinemas and new ideas arrive, while old habits stay put.

{{story:indian-railways}}

John Harvey Kellogg was a doctor who ran the Battle Creek Sanitarium, a large health resort. He had many strict ideas about diet, some sensible and some very odd. His younger brother, Will Keith Kellogg, managed the business side.

In 1894, according to the family story, a pot of cooked wheat was left standing for many hours. When the brothers rolled it, each grain came out as a separate flake, which could be toasted crisp. Patients loved them. Corn flakes followed a few years later, and in 1906 Will started his own company to sell them to the public. Against his brother's wishes, he added sugar.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}
